body:
The **Stem Player** was a $200, palm-sized disc developed by Kano Computing under the YEEZY TECH brand and launched alongside Kanye West's *Donda* album in August 2021. Its seven-centimeter body held four capacitive LED sliders that let listeners isolate and blend *audio stems* — vocals, drums, bass, and samples — separated in real time by onboard machine learning. The collaboration ended in January 2023 after West's antisemitic statements attracted broad condemnation; Kano Computing entered administration months later, and founder Alex Klein re-acquired the assets through Ashdust LLP, which continues developing hardware successors under the Stem brand.

@ty_kra_lab claims to have rebuilt a working prototype of the Stem Player interface in under 48 hours, working with the AI mini-app platform **Wabi** — founded by Eugenia Kuyda, creator of the AI companion app **Replika**, and backed by a $20 million November 2025 pre-seed from investors including Andreessen Horowitz. The technical centerpiece: the device's entire physical form — every curve, speaker aperture, and glowing LED indicator — is rendered using *signed distance fields* and *raymarching* inside a single *fragment shader*, with no polygon meshes or external model files of any kind.

*Signed distance fields* are mathematical functions that return, for any point in space, its shortest distance to the nearest surface — negative inside the object, positive outside, zero on the surface. Complex forms are assembled by combining simple geometric primitives (spheres, cylinders, toruses) with Boolean logic. *Raymarching* — also called sphere tracing — pairs this with the GPU: a ray steps through space pixel by pixel, using the SDF's output as a guaranteed-safe step size until it converges on a surface and lighting is applied. The entire loop runs inside a *fragment shader*, the small GPU program that fires once per screen pixel. Graphics researcher Inigo Quilez, co-creator of the browser platform Shadertoy, has catalogued the canonical SDF primitive library; a circular consumer-electronics disc like the Stem Player maps neatly to a handful of them. This rendering approach traces its roots to the *demoscene*, a competitive computational-art tradition that has been producing feature-quality 3D environments inside 64-kilobyte executables since the early 2010s — no mesh files of any kind.

@ty_kra_lab labels the practice “system sculpting” rather than *vibe coding* — Andrej Karpathy's February 2025 coinage, named Collins Dictionary's Word of the Year 2025, for the loop of natural-language prompting an AI, running the output, and iterating without reading the underlying code. The distinction is more rhetorical than procedural: system sculpting implies intentional architectural thinking alongside AI generation, but the underlying loop is the same. The 48-hour timeline for a visual prototype is well within documented vibe-coding norms. What is less clear is what “fully working” encompasses. The Stem Player's defining feature is on-device *audio stem separation* — isolating vocals from a full mix in real time. Nothing in the post confirms that this machine-learning pipeline was rebuilt in the sprint; a prototype that delivers interactive controls over pre-separated or simulated audio would be a credible but substantially more modest achievement.

The original device carried unusual cultural weight, arriving as a hardware-locked distribution format — West released *Donda* exclusively to Stem Player owners for an initial period — and demonstrating that a physical object could double as a media platform. That a developer in 2026 can sketch a credible interactive approximation of that interface in two days, using AI scaffolding and GPU-native procedural geometry with zero asset dependencies, shows how sharply the gap between design concept and interactive prototype has collapsed. Whether this build is a demo or a genuinely usable artifact is secondary to what it signals about the tools.
